      "fullDescription": "The blue spotlight hits the stage and suddenly you're in Memphis. The pompadour, the curled lip, the stance — but the voice that emerges carries something else, something that learned rock and roll through Greek speakers and Athenian nights.\n\nDoros Dimosthenous has spent years channeling Elvis Presley, not as impersonation but as conversation. When a Greek singer inhabits the King's catalog, something strange and wonderful happens. The songs that defined American rebellion — \"Suspicious Minds,\" \"Can't Help Falling in Love,\" \"Jailhouse Rock\" — absorb Mediterranean inflection, the warmth of a culture that understood passion before rock and roll named it.\n\nHalf Note Jazz Club provides the setting this tribute deserves. Since 1979, this Mets institution has hosted jazz royalty, and tonight it bends its programming to honor a different kind of royalty. The room holds maybe 200 people, close enough to catch the performer's expression, intimate enough that Elvis's ballads fill the space without straining. The jazz club acoustic treats the voice as the instrument it is — every vibrato, every dramatic pause, every moment of surrender audible.\n\nThe crowd mixes unexpectedly. Elvis devotees who've seen every tribute share tables with Half Note regulars curious about the venue's departure from jazz. Greeks who grew up on rock and roll's international wave. Couples who fell in love to songs that Doros is about to deliver. The pre-show energy carries affection — people ready to be transported, permission already granted.\n\nIf you need pure authenticity, if only Elvis himself will do, tribute is always translation. Doros channels rather than copies, and the Greek angle adds rather than subtracts. But if you've been searching for an evening where the King's songbook meets Half Note's warmth, where rock and roll romance gets the intimate treatment it deserves — tonight, the blue suede shoes dance in Mets.\n\n| Info | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Date** | Saturday, February 21, 2026 |\n| **Venue** | Half Note Jazz Club |\n| **Address** | Trivonianou 17, Mets |\n| **Getting there** | Akropoli Metro (Red line), 10-minute walk |\n| **Price** | Table: 15-20 euro, Bar: 10-15 euro |\n| **Reservations** | Essential - call ahead, arrive 20 min early |\n\nElvis left the building decades ago. Tonight at Half Note, his songs find new rooms.",
